---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
import { getSitePage, REVIEWED_DATE, REVIEWED_ISO } from "../data/sitePages";

const page = getSitePage("/shipping-delivery/");
---

<BaseLayout
  title={page.title}
  description={page.description}
  alternateMd="/shipping-delivery/index.md"
  pageType="WebPage"
  dateModified={REVIEWED_ISO}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Shipping & delivery" }]} />
  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h1>Shipping and delivery</h1>
      <p class="small">Last reviewed: {REVIEWED_DATE}</p>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        Production and transit are separate clocks. We control the production plan until the goods are handed to the
        carrier; after that, the route and the customs process determine the rest. The quotation states which clock
        and which shipping term it is pricing.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Production and transit are separate</h2>
      <p>
        A label order cannot ship before the proof is approved and the deposit is received. The production estimate
        starts from approval, not from the first email. Transit starts from collection, and customs can add time that
        neither we nor the carrier controls. If a date is essential, tell us before the quote so the production and
        freight options can be planned together.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Production versus transit</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">Estimated time</th><th scope="col">Starts when</th><th scope="col">What affects it</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Print production</td><td>7–12 working days</td><td>Artwork approval, deposit and material availability</td><td>Quantity, finishing, material, colour proof and press scheduling</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sample production</td><td>5–8 working days plus transit</td><td>Sample specification, artwork approval and sample payment</td><td>Material, handwork, die and courier route</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Express transit</td><td>Typically 3–7 working days to the US, UK or EU</td><td>Carrier collection</td><td>Destination, service, customs clearance and peak season</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Air or sea freight</td><td>Quoted per shipment</td><td>Carrier booking and export clearance</td><td>Weight, volume, route, port congestion and import clearance</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        These are planning ranges, not guaranteed dates. A carrier or customs authority can change the estimate after
        collection. We will tell you when we know of a delay rather than let a tracking page be the only source.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Incoterms® 2020 in a quotation</h2>
      <p>
        The quotation must state one shipping term. EXW means the buyer collects at the named seller location; FOB
        means the seller delivers to the named port and clears export, with the main carriage and import arranged by
        the buyer; DAP means we deliver to the named destination but the buyer remains responsible for import duty
        and clearance; DDP means we arrange delivery with duty paid, subject to the destination and the quote. These
        are options that can be priced, not promises that every route is available. The named place is part of the
        term, and any insurance, unloading or clearance cost not included remains the buyer's.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Duties, taxes and HS codes</h2>
      <p>
        Import duty and VAT are set by the destination country. They are not included unless the quotation says DDP.
        We provide commercial-invoice information and the HS code used for the shipment; we do not issue a binding
        classification ruling. Your customs broker or importer remains responsible for classification, licensing and
        duty. If you have a tax number for the UK or EU, send it with the order so the invoice can show the correct
        details.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Packaging and split shipments</h2>
      <p>
        Labels usually travel on rolls or flat sheets, boxed and wrapped against moisture. Stickers may be flat or on
        rolls depending on the die and quantity. Retail-ready packing, individual poly bags and special carton marks
        can be quoted, but they change packing time and freight. If one order goes to several addresses, each is a
        separate shipment with its own cost and delivery estimate; tell us before the quote if the order needs to be
        split.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Tracking and delivery problems</h2>
      <p>
        We provide the tracking reference supplied by the carrier. Tracking can pause while a parcel is in customs or
        moving between carrier networks; a scan gap is not proof of loss. If tracking has not updated for an
        unreasonable period, send us the order reference and we will ask the carrier to investigate. If an address
        is incomplete or the consignee refuses the parcel, return or re-delivery costs are quoted before the
        replacement is sent.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Damage and short delivery claims</h2>
      <p>
        Report transit damage or a short delivery within 7 days of delivery. Photograph the outer carton, the
        packaging label and the affected goods, then email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> with the
        order reference, quantity affected and the batch or lot reference. We will raise the carrier claim and
        confirm the remedy in writing. Production defects have a 30-day inspection period under the
        <a href="/terms/">terms</a>; read the <a href="/refund-policy/">returns policy</a> for how reprints and
        refunds are handled.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        Related policies: <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> · <a href="/cookies/">Cookies</a> ·
        <a href="/terms/">Terms</a> · <a href="/refund-policy/">Returns</a> ·
        <a href="/declarations/">Declarations</a> · <a href="/editorial-standards/">Editorial standards</a> ·
        <a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a> · <a href="/corrections/">Corrections</a> ·
        <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a> · <a href="/accessibility/">Accessibility</a> ·
        <a href="/contact/">Contact</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
